<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Group as Closure

> Status: PROPOSED — design intent, mostly unbuilt. The keyhive membership CRDT
> (delegate/revoke) stands; the threshold composition and the eviction stack ride
> ahead of code.

A group holds no central object. A group names the ''closure'' over signed individual
edges — each member declares their own edge, the group emerges as the reciprocal-edge
fan, ''evaluated'' as a query, never instantiated as a thing a single key owns. Four
prior-arts converge on this shape; this meme stands them whole and maps them onto the
`#has`-stack.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/core-claim>>

!! A Group Names a Closure, Not an Object

Each member ''declares their own signed, typed, `lar:`-named edge'' — //"I place X in//
//cabal-C with role R"// — and signs it down their own key. No edge speaks for another
member; no doc holds a roster a single key may rewrite. The group ''resolves'' as the
reciprocal-edge closure: the set of edges that mutually name each other, gathered by a
query a vessel runs over the edges it has synced.

SPKI/SDSI carries this whole — //no group object exists;//
//groups stand as relational constructs built from distributed name bindings//, and
linked-local-names grant ''global reach without a global namespace''. The group reads off
the edges, never out of a register.

''Causal-island-safe by construction.'' A closure ''evaluated'' never demands a global
now (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/federated-causal-islands>>). Each vessel
resolves the closure over ''its own synced view'' — //"the group, as of my last sync"// —
and two vessels may hold different views without either lying. The edges glue (or fail to
glue) as a presheaf; the membership query reads the local section. No coordinator
materializes a canonical roster, because no canonical roster exists to materialize.

''STATED POSITIVELY, so a later hand cannot ship a cache that breaks nothing written here'': there exists ''no member list to leak — only held faces and the edges between them''. The person never entered the data, so the roster's absence reads as a fact about what the model CAN hold rather than a policy about what it chooses to expose.

''Which sorts what may cross.'' //Publish// packs four acts: the DECLARATION that a partition stands, the METHOD by which a reading gets computed, and the READING itself MAY cross at a chosen tier; ''the INPUTS never do''. Rival readings that disagree stay checkable without any party holding the edge set, which is how the split survives publication at all.

''The edge carries the verb.'' An edge names what a member //does// — //places · vouches ·//
//delegates · scopes// — never an essence a member //holds-as-identity//. A membership reads
as a live, signed, decaying gesture, not a row in a table.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/quorum>>

!! Quorum — a Composed Threshold Subject

A quorum names no primitive. A quorum ''composes'' as a threshold subject — SPKI's
`(k-of-n s1 s2 … sn)` — where each of K members ''signs independently down their own''
''chain'', and the verifier counts K satisfied chains. The threshold subject stands as one
more principal in the closure, addressable like any member.

Three properties ride this composition load-bearing:

* ''Transparent.'' Who-signed stays visible — the verifier sees //which// K chains
  satisfied, never an anonymized count. This fits Plurality Pono: the house surfaces
  competing readings rather than blending them, and a quorum surfaces //who// carried the
  threshold (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot#/voice-house>>).
* ''Nesting.'' A satisfied quorum stands as a subject another quorum may name —
  quorum-of-quorums — so a cabal-of-cabals threshold composes without new machinery.
* ''Composed, never granted.'' Keyhive supplies ''membership'' (the delegate/revoke
  CRDT) and ''deliberately no quorum''; the threshold rides //above// keyhive, assembled
  from independent signatures the verifier counts. the
  layering keeps the floor (membership) convergent and the ceiling (threshold) explicit.

A K-of-N threshold defeats the single-key capture a central group-object would invite:
no one signature, and no one stolen key, moves the subject.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/eviction>>

!! Eviction — a Kāpae on the Membership Stack

Removing a member raises a ''kāpae'' on the membership stack — a remove-wins tombstone
that ''shadows'' the member's prior edges where the cascade resolves
(<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/membership-sense>>; the kāpae model
stands whole there — the membership-stack eviction, remove-wins-under-partition, and
re-admit-by-lowering — this meme references it, never re-defines it; the bag-cascade
shadow rides <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-model#/anti-patterns>>).
Eviction composes three moves, none of them a state-replay:

* ''kāpae (remove-wins shadow)'' — the convergent CRDT remove, last-writer-shadows, so a
  re-added edge does not silently resurrect a removed member.
* ''observed-remove'' — the remove names the exact edges it observed, so concurrent
  re-adds the remover never saw survive (OR-Set discipline).
* ''key-rotation'' — the read-set rotates its epoch forward, so the evicted key reads no
  future tail (forward-only revocation, the epoch-lease, never a targeted global undo).

the stack DOES NOT re-roll Matrix-style state-resolution
replay — no group walks an event DAG to recompute a canonical membership. The closure
re-evaluates over the current edges; the kāpae shadows; the epoch rolls; the verdict
falls out of the query, local-first.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/maps-to-stack>>

!! Maps to Our Stack

The closure model carries directly onto the existing identity architecture
(<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity>>):

* ''PersonaGroup = a closure over vessels.'' One operator's N device-vessels resolve as
  the reciprocal-edge closure of per-vessel delegation edges — never a roster doc a single
  vessel owns. The device-admit ceremony adds an edge; it instantiates no central object.
* ''ONE PRIMITIVE, not two kinds — the Circle and the Cabal run the same closure.'' A
  handle-Circle (a human's own vessels) and a cabal-realm (mates mutually holding a charter)
  differ in ''what the edges name'' — a `handle ↔ DID` binding versus a maintenance/charter
  edge — and in ''how many ordering authorities'' the closure spans. Nothing else. Both
  resolve as the reciprocal-edge fan, evaluated locally, instantiating no central object
  (`persona-circle`: //"a Circle IS what mutual bindings declare"//; `cabal-realm`:
  //"authority is the closure of mutual maintenance edges"//). The single ⊥ multi principal
  count names no KIND difference — it decides exactly one thing: whether a ''tie-break'' is
  owed (single-principal takes none, //you// standing as the one ordering authority;
  multi-principal takes a kahu the closure constitutes for itself, or accept-and-heal).
  Read them as ''one primitive parameterised'', never as two ontological tiers.
* ''Plurality = one human → many personas.'' A quorum across //personas// (not bodies)
  enacts Plurality Pono at the credential layer: the operator's multitudes each sign their
  own chain, and a threshold across them carries a decision the way the Voice house carries
  a multi-Voice turn — disagreement stays visible.
* ''Rides `#has`-stack composition.'' A group composes by ''added edges'', never by
  subclassing — the nameless-entity + signed-edge ontology, no group //type//, only the
  closure over what each member `#has` declared
  (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/social-axis>>).
* ''The Kowloon Admins role-circle = the keeper-cabal skeleton.'' The Circles
  role-of-admins (mutual-petname recognition, no central registrar) adapts web2→web3 as
  the keeper-cabal: Automerge for the edge log, UCAN for the attenuated delegation, `#has`
  for the composition, threshold for the quorum. The role names a closure, the circle
  names its members' mutual recognition.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prior-art>>

!! Prior Art — Four Converging Roots

four independent traditions arrive at //group = relation,//
//not object//:

* ''SPKI/SDSI'' (Ellison, Rivest, Lampson) — //no group object exists; groups stand as//
//  relational constructs from distributed name bindings//; `(k-of-n …)` threshold subjects;
  linked local names reach globally without a global namespace.
* ''Diaspora aspects'' — each user sorts their own contacts into private, asymmetric
  //aspects//; no shared group object, the audience resolves per-author at share time — a
  closure evaluated, not a roster owned.
* ''Kowloon Circles'' — role-circles of mutual petnames, no central registrar; membership
  reads off who-recognizes-whom.
* ''Object-capability (ocap)'' — authority rides the held edge (the capability), not a
  membership in an ambient list; designation carries authority, defeating the confused
  deputy.

The convergence reads load-bearing: when four traditions across cryptography, social
software, fiction-grounded design, and capability security independently dissolve the
group-object, the closure model carries a real pattern integrity, not a stylistic choice.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

